      "why": "The thesis of the talk stated in one line."
    },
    {
      "text": "Internally, we call this gap happening right now between reasoning and search the knowledge gap. And it's not just one obscure theory of ours. We see that actually with real benchmarks and real tasks that this gap exists in real world as well.",
      "timestamp_sec": 48,
      "why": "Names the central concept and stakes the empirical claim behind it."
    },
    {
      "text": "Oracle means what is the maximum theoretical performance of the models if you would put in the right documents with the question",
      "timestamp_sec": 91,
      "why": "Defines the measurement device the entire argument rests on."
    },
    {
      "text": "We see for Office for BrowseComp it's 93% and for Office QA Pro it's 64%.",
      "timestamp_sec": 145,
      "why": "The Oracle ceiling numbers."
    },
    {
      "text": "And you see there's a sharp drop in the quality of the answers Codex produces. For BrowseComp it's nine points and for Office QA Pro it's eight points.",
      "timestamp_sec": 145,
      "why": "The headline gap: near-total collapse from Oracle to default agent tooling."
    },
    {
      "text": "So we see that the models are extremely capable if they would get the right documents but if you put them into the noisy corpus the performance drops sharply. Meaning that actually the bottleneck here is not the reasoning. It's actually the access to the right knowledge it needs to answer this question.",
      "timestamp_sec": 145,
      "why": "The load-bearing position — reasoning is not the constraint."
    },
    {
      "text": "So for BrowseComp the difference between the Oracle and the uh GPT-5 with Mixbread is just three point and for Office QA Pro we even almost completely closed the gap.",
      "timestamp_sec": 194,
      "why": "Quantifies how much of the gap is recoverable by retrieval alone."
    },
    {
      "text": "here's an example query we found uh during some benchmarking, which is senator woman questions billionaires not a company then okay thank you staff will check hearing.",
      "timestamp_sec": 194,
      "why": "Concrete artifact of the failure mode, more persuasive than the abstraction."
    },
    {
      "text": "And the reason why the models write these type of queries is they're mostly trained for coding task, like for coding agents, which are then optimized for code base exploration using tools like grep.",
      "timestamp_sec": 245,
      "why": "Causal explanation blaming coding-agent training for bad retrieval behavior."
    },
    {
      "text": "Most benchmarks we have right now, like Beer, Nano Beer, use Caveman's style queries, which are entity-based queries that structurally favor heavily BM25.",
      "timestamp_sec": 292,
      "why": "A direct criticism of standard retrieval benchmarks others rely on."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, right now, the agent guesses the keywords to actually increase the overlap between the query and documents and can't really use powerful search tools properly.",
      "timestamp_sec": 292,
      "why": "Summarizes the behavioral diagnosis the rest of the talk fixes."
    },
    {
      "text": "Um and to work beyond code search for knowledge work. And most importantly, the agent should be precise, fast, and cheap.",
      "timestamp_sec": 292,
      "why": "States the design objectives that justify the small-model choice."
    },
    {
      "text": "This is used as a very wide semantic search where the agent receives up to 50 retrieve chunks. Um and it sees only summaries of the chunk contents to really have just like an overview of the corpus",
      "timestamp_sec": 343,
      "why": "Specific context-budget tradeoff in tool design."
    },
    {
      "text": "That's why we decided to um define that it has a maximum four search rounds, but within each search round it can have parallel searches.",
      "timestamp_sec": 385,
      "why": "The concrete latency-versus-exploration tradeoff in the agent loop."
    },
    {
      "text": "The agent has to articulate articulate what evidence it needs before writing the query.",
      "timestamp_sec": 495,
      "why": "Goal framing as a prompt-level mechanism for better queries."
    },
    {
      "text": "We kind of trick the um model into not thinking it has to write the typical BM25 base query by just instructing it to write one concise sentence describing what it wants to find, instead of directly instructing write a search query, so it cannot fall into this old pattern.",
      "timestamp_sec": 545,
      "why": "The single most transferable prompt trick in the talk."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, the first step in training is um supervised fine-tuning with a larger teacher LLM. And then we uh did on-policy reinforcement learning with an own search reward. Our search reward is a combination of both of a retrieval reward and a trajectory reward.",
      "timestamp_sec": 599,
      "why": "The training recipe in compact form."
    },
    {
      "text": "Of course, like we have rubrics that are where the judge is deciding if the query is really a natural sentence. Also, if the amount of exploration is sufficient, is it too much or too less.",
      "timestamp_sec": 702,
      "why": "Shows query style and exploration budget being directly rewarded, not just outcomes."
    },
    {
      "text": "It's really a sentence describing what it wants to find and not a weird keywordy added behind each other formulation.",
      "timestamp_sec": 753,
      "why": "The observable behavior change the whole pipeline was built to produce."
    },
    {
      "text": "Our trained agent is not released yet, unfortunately. However, we have some intermediate results.",
      "timestamp_sec": 753,
      "why": "Important caveat for anyone weighing whether they can use this."
    },
    {
      "text": "we see that we achieved an NDCG of 10 at 10 of 0.4, which is a huge jump um towards the model that performed best on the paper of this benchmark, which is the GPT multi-hop agent",
      "timestamp_sec": 753,
      "why": "The headline retrieval-quality result versus a named baseline."
    },
    {
      "text": "And this agent is top one on the snowflakes match QA benchmark, achieving an accuracy of 93.4 when we give the Gemini 3.5 flash model our genetic search as search tool.",
      "timestamp_sec": 804,
      "why": "The production result on a public leaderboard, with the model pairing named."
    }
